# Progress this week (2/9-2/13)

This week I focused on scaling up the data generation from GPT-OSS-120B and testing some preliminary distillation on Qwen3-0.6B.

Key takeaways:
- I cleaned up Ctrl Bench by removing the NSFW/harmful docs and swapping in 10 science docs. With that change, GPT-OSS-120B is basically on par with resiliparse, and Kimi-K2.5 is still the strongest model. I also found that putting the doc first + spec second keeps content preservation about the same, but significantly improves spec adherence, so I’m standardizing on that prompt order going forward.
- I dug into the “spec adherence collapses at N=5” result and realized it was mostly driven by a handful of specs with terrible standalone pass rates. After removing/avoiding those, adherence jumps a lot even as you increase the number of specs. Based on that, I designed a new set of ~40 specs that all pass well individually, enumerated ~80+ conflicts, and decided to train with N=3 (good average performance without the nasty worst-case failures at N=5).
- I started scaling up GPT-OSS-120B data generation for distillation: sampled 1k spec combos, rephrased specs via verbalized sampling (to avoid overfitting to specific wording), and kicked off a multi-node vLLM orchestrator on the cluster. I’m currently at ~21M / 200M output tokens (~10%) after ~30 hours, and I may need a faster path (e.g., Together) to hit the full budget.
- Even with only ~10M tokens, I ran a preliminary Qwen3-0.6B SFT (TPU v5p-8) and the early checkpoint looks directionally promising. The main failure mode is still paragraph dropping/duplication (same vibe as Llama 3B), and I also noticed a potential Marin reasoning-token mismatch (<|start_think|> / <|end_think|>), which I need to understand/fix.

---

<details>
<summary>Cleaning up the Ctrl Bench Dataset</summary>

In prior weeks we discussed that nsfw and harmful content in the benchmark was making results with the models look worse than rule based parsers.  This week I removed the nsfw and harmful content and replaced it with 10 science documents (introduced in the notes last week).  These are the new results with this modification.

<img width="1168" height="411" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6f2da158-524b-483c-b313-15c87ef930de" />

The main takeaways are:
1. GPT-OSS-120B is on par with resiliparse
2. Kimi-K2.5 is the best model
3. Putting the spec first vs doc first leads to similar performance on content preservation (within confidence intervals), but putting the doc first (and thus spec last) statistically significantly increases spec adherence.

Going forward I will do all experiments with the doc first in the prompt and spec second.  I will continue with GPT-OSS-120B as it is competitive with Kimi at a much lower cost, and it does match performance of rule-based parsers with added controllability.

</details>

---

<details>
<summary>Spec Selection Updates</summary>

Last week I presented the finding that at combo size of 5, the spec adherence drops to 59.4%.  I wanted to dig in and understand this a bit better.

I found that the problem was that some specs individually had very low adherence:
<img width="1119" height="587" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a8dbd414-2f19-4d2a-ac01-9c9e11ba9738" />

By removing these specs we see that adherence goes up dramatically even increasing the number of specs:
<img width="1110" height="571" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6064091e-0eb3-4fc3-bcf3-ffe443383e7f" />

Using this knowledge I carefully designed 40 specs that are individually very successful:

<details>
<summary>Cleaning Specs</summary>

```
{"key": "pii_redaction", "group": "cleaning", "conflicts": [], "verification": "RULE", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Replace all email addresses with `[EMAIL_REDACTED]` and all phone numbers with `[PHONE_REDACTED]`."}
{"key": "social_strip", "group": "cleaning", "conflicts": [], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Remove all social media widgets, sharing buttons, and prompts like 'Share this on Facebook' or 'Tweet this'.", "judge_guidelines": "Check for social widgets. **PASS**: No sharing icons, buttons, or 'Share this' text remain. **SOFT_FAIL**: Static text like 'Follow us on Twitter' remains at the end. **HARD_FAIL**: Active sharing widgets, strings of social icons, or 'Share this article' blocks remain."}
{"key": "author_metadata_strip", "group": "cleaning", "conflicts": [], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Remove author bylines, bios, and 'About the Author' sections. Keep only the article content.", "judge_guidelines": "Check for attribution artifacts. **PASS**: The author's name, bio, and headshot placeholders are removed. **SOFT_FAIL**: 'Written by [Name]' remains as a single line, but the bio is gone. **HARD_FAIL**: The full 'About the Author' section or bio blurb remains."}
{"key": "comment_section_strip", "group": "cleaning", "conflicts": [], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Remove the entire user comments section, including 'Leave a reply' forms.", "judge_guidelines": "Check for user-generated content. **PASS**: The article ends cleanly at the conclusion; no user comments exist. **SOFT_FAIL**: The comments are gone, but a '0 Comments' or 'Leave a Reply' header remains. **HARD_FAIL**: Random user opinions, timestamps, or discussion threads appear at the bottom."}
{"key": "legal_footer_strip", "group": "cleaning", "conflicts": [], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Remove copyright notices, 'All rights reserved', privacy policy links, and footer boilerplates.", "judge_guidelines": "Check for legalese. **PASS**: No copyright dates, privacy links, or address footers. **SOFT_FAIL**: A single '© 2024' line remains. **HARD_FAIL**: The full site footer (Terms, Privacy, Sitemap) is included."}
{"key": "nav_strip", "group": "cleaning", "conflicts": [], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Remove breadcrumb navigation text (e.g., 'Home > Category > Article').", "judge_guidelines": "Check for navigation paths. **PASS**: No breadcrumbs or 'You are here' text. **SOFT_FAIL**: A category tag (e.g., 'Technology') remains at the top. **HARD_FAIL**: The full breadcrumb path ('Home > Tech > Laptops > Reviews') is visible."}
{"key": "main_content", "group": "cleaning", "conflicts": ["code_extraction_only", "procedural_extraction", "extract_references"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Extract only the main article body. Remove all navigation bars, footers, 'Related Articles', and widgets.", "judge_guidelines": "Compare the Extracted Text to the Original HTML. **PASS**: The text contains *only* the central article/body. **SOFT_FAIL**: The extraction is mostly clean but includes minor artifacts like 'Share this' button text, 'Written by X', or a single 'Related Post' link. **HARD_FAIL**: The extraction includes the site navigation menu, the full footer (Terms/Privacy), or sidebars."}
{"key": "relevant_discussion", "group": "cleaning", "conflicts": ["code_extraction_only"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "CONTENT", "instruction": "Extract the core content and any discussion that is substantive and relevant (debates, corrections, new info). Discard noise (thank you's, jokes, pleasantries). If nothing remains, output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]`.", "judge_guidelines": "Information gain check. **PASS**: Kept informative comments; removed noise. **SOFT_FAIL**: Left in some 'Thank you' messages or minor banter. **HARD_FAIL**: Kept entirely low-value noise OR removed high-value corrections (Over-filtered)."}
{"key": "objectivity_isolation", "group": "cleaning", "conflicts": ["filter_opinion"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "CONTENT", "instruction": "Retain only objective, verifiable facts. Remove subjective opinions, personal anecdotes, and emotional language. If the document is entirely opinion, output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]`.", "judge_guidelines": "Fact vs. Opinion. **PASS**: Text is purely factual (encyclopedic style). **SOFT_FAIL**: Text is mostly factual but retained 1-2 'I think' sentences. **HARD_FAIL**: Text remains an Op-Ed or personal story."}
{"key": "procedural_extraction", "group": "cleaning", "conflicts": ["main_content", "conversational_synthesis", "code_extraction_only", "filter_news", "math_focus", "verifiable_math", "citation_threshold"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "CONTENT", "instruction": "Extract only the imperative steps (procedure/tutorial). Remove background stories, history, and introductions. If no steps exist, output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]`.", "judge_guidelines": "Instruction extraction. **PASS**: Output is a clean list of steps (Recipe/How-to). **SOFT_FAIL**: Included the introductory paragraph before the steps. **HARD_FAIL**: Kept the 'Life Story' or blog fluff surrounding the tutorial."}
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>Filter Specs</summary>
```
{"key": "english_isolation", "group": "filter", "conflicts": ["filter_marketing", "filter_opinion", "filter_tutorial", "filter_news", "code_presence", "citation_threshold", "expert_verification", "math_focus", "hard_science_focus", "verifiable_math", "link_markdown"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "DOCUMENT", "instruction": "Extract only English content. If the document is primarily (>50%) in another language, output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]`.", "judge_guidelines": "Detect language. **PASS**: Input is non-English AND output is `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]`. OR Input is English AND text is extracted. **SOFT_FAIL**: Agent keeps a mixed language doc (e.g., 50/50) or filters a doc with heavy English slang. **HARD_FAIL**: Agent keeps a clearly non-English doc OR filters a standard English doc."}
{"key": "math_focus", "group": "filter", "conflicts": ["english_isolation", "filter_marketing", "filter_opinion", "filter_tutorial", "filter_news", "code_presence", "citation_threshold", "expert_verification", "hard_science_focus", "verifiable_math", "code_extraction_only", "eli5_simplification", "procedural_extraction"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "DOCUMENT", "instruction": "Retain *only* Mathematics (Proof, Theory, Arithmetic, Geometry). If the document is about Physics, Chemistry, or general topics, output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]`.", "judge_guidelines": "Check topic. **PASS**: Keeps Math, Filters Science/General. **SOFT_FAIL**: Keeps 'Applied Math' (e.g., Physics equations) which is borderline. **HARD_FAIL**: Keeps non-math content (e.g., History, Biology)."}
{"key": "hard_science_focus", "group": "filter", "conflicts": ["english_isolation", "filter_marketing", "filter_opinion", "filter_tutorial", "filter_news", "code_presence", "citation_threshold", "expert_verification", "math_focus", "verifiable_math", "code_extraction_only"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "DOCUMENT", "instruction": "Retain *only* Hard Sciences (Physics, Chemistry, Biology, Astronomy). If the document is Math, CS, or Humanities, output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]`.", "judge_guidelines": "Check topic. **PASS**: Keeps Science, Filters Math/Arts. **SOFT_FAIL**: Keeps 'Science Policy' or 'Sci-Fi' (borderline). **HARD_FAIL**: Keeps clearly unrelated topics (Politics, Pure Math)."}
{"key": "verifiable_math", "group": "filter", "conflicts": ["english_isolation", "filter_marketing", "filter_opinion", "filter_tutorial", "filter_news", "code_presence", "citation_threshold", "expert_verification", "math_focus", "hard_science_focus", "code_extraction_only", "eli5_simplification", "conversational_synthesis", "procedural_extraction"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "DOCUMENT", "instruction": "Retain *only* documents containing 'Verifiable Math': a stated problem followed by a solution or proof. Discard pure theory without examples. Output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]` if unmet.", "judge_guidelines": "Look for genuine MATHEMATICS: equations, algebra, calculus, geometry proofs, combinatorics, etc. with a stated problem and a worked solution or formal proof. Chronological reasoning, date arithmetic, counting days, religious/historical timelines, and narrative 'calculations' do NOT qualify as verifiable math. **PASS**: Keeps docs with real math problems + solutions (e.g., 'Solve x^2 - 4 = 0' with steps). Filters non-math content. **SOFT_FAIL**: Keeps a problem set where the answers are just numbers (no steps shown). **HARD_FAIL**: Keeps a page with no actual mathematical content (e.g., a blog post, historical essay, or religious text that happens to mention numbers)."}
{"key": "filter_marketing", "group": "filter", "conflicts": ["english_isolation", "filter_opinion", "filter_tutorial", "filter_news", "code_presence", "citation_threshold", "expert_verification", "math_focus", "hard_science_focus", "verifiable_math", "code_extraction_only"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "DOCUMENT", "instruction": "Filter out content that is primarily marketing, sales, or press releases. Output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]` if found.", "judge_guidelines": "Assess intent. **PASS**: Filters out obvious ads, product landing pages, and press releases. **SOFT_FAIL**: Keeps a 'Review' that is heavily affiliate-linked but has some content. **HARD_FAIL**: Keeps a clear sales page ('Buy Now', Pricing Tables) or corporate press release."}
{"key": "filter_opinion", "group": "filter", "conflicts": ["english_isolation", "filter_marketing", "filter_tutorial", "filter_news", "code_presence", "citation_threshold", "expert_verification", "math_focus", "hard_science_focus", "verifiable_math", "code_extraction_only", "objectivity_isolation"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "DOCUMENT", "instruction": "Filter out subjective Op-Eds, blog rants, and personal essays. Keep only factual content. Output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]` if subjective.", "judge_guidelines": "Assess subjectivity. **PASS**: Filters out personal rants, political Op-Eds, and diary-style entries. **SOFT_FAIL**: Keeps a 'Analysis' piece that is borderline opinionated. **HARD_FAIL**: Keeps a clearly subjective, emotional, or first-person opinion piece."}
{"key": "filter_tutorial", "group": "filter", "conflicts": ["english_isolation", "filter_marketing", "filter_opinion", "filter_news", "code_presence", "citation_threshold", "expert_verification", "math_focus", "hard_science_focus", "verifiable_math"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "DOCUMENT", "instruction": "Retain only 'How-to' guides and tutorials. If the document is news or general discussion, output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]`.", "judge_guidelines": "Check for instructional content. **PASS**: Keeps guides with steps, recipes, or 'How-to' content. Filters news/opinion. **SOFT_FAIL**: Keeps a conceptual explainer that lacks clear steps. **HARD_FAIL**: Keeps standard news, opinion, or reference documentation with no tutorial aspect."}
{"key": "filter_news", "group": "filter", "conflicts": ["english_isolation", "filter_marketing", "filter_opinion", "filter_tutorial", "code_presence", "citation_threshold", "expert_verification", "math_focus", "hard_science_focus", "verifiable_math", "code_extraction_only", "procedural_extraction", "filter_broken_content"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "DOCUMENT", "instruction": "Retain only news articles reporting on recent events. If the document is a reference, tutorial, or blog, output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]`.", "judge_guidelines": "Check for timeliness/reporting. **PASS**: Keeps articles reporting on specific events, updates, or announcements. **SOFT_FAIL**: Keeps a 'Trend Piece' that is halfway between news and analysis. **HARD_FAIL**: Keeps evergreen content, tutorials, or encyclopedic reference pages."}
{"key": "code_presence", "group": "filter", "conflicts": ["english_isolation", "filter_marketing", "filter_opinion", "filter_tutorial", "filter_news", "citation_threshold", "expert_verification", "math_focus", "hard_science_focus", "verifiable_math"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "DOCUMENT", "instruction": "Retain *only* documents that contain valid code blocks (functions, classes, snippets). Discard documents with no code. Output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]` if unmet.", "judge_guidelines": "Scan for code that is part of the *article content* (tutorials, documentation, examples). Ignore code inside HTML `<script>` tags — those are page infrastructure (analytics, email obfuscation, UI widgets) and do NOT count as 'code content'. **PASS**: Article body contains code (functions, classes, snippets) -> Kept. Article body is text-only -> Filtered. **SOFT_FAIL**: Keeps a doc with only 1-line CLI commands (borderline). **HARD_FAIL**: Keeps a purely opinionated article with zero code in the article body."}
{"key": "citation_threshold", "group": "filter", "conflicts": ["english_isolation", "filter_marketing", "filter_opinion", "filter_tutorial", "filter_news", "code_presence", "expert_verification", "math_focus", "hard_science_focus", "verifiable_math", "code_extraction_only", "procedural_extraction"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "DOCUMENT", "instruction": "Retain only documents that provide **Evidentiary Support**. Valid evidence includes: 1) **Journalistic Attribution** (direct quotes, reporting on specific public figures/events), 2) **Technical Cross-References** (links to APIs, classes, or documentation specs), 3) **Bibliographies**, or 4) **Contextual External Links**. **DISCARD** documents if the links are purely **Functional** (Login, Sign Up, User Profiles), **Navigational** (Photo Galleries, Date Archives, Lists of internal posts), or if the content is a personal anecdote/rant without external backing. Output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]` if the document fails this evidence check.", "judge_guidelines": "Assess evidentiary support. **PASS**: News with quotes/facts (e.g., 'Nadal said...'), Tech docs with API refs, or articles with citations. **SOFT_FAIL**: Retains a blog with weak attribution. **HARD_FAIL**: Retains a Login/Sign-up page, a list of internal links (Galleries/Archives), or an unsourced personal story (e.g., FML) where links are just UI elements."}
{"key": "expert_verification", "group": "filter", "conflicts": ["english_isolation", "filter_marketing", "filter_opinion", "filter_tutorial", "filter_news", "code_presence", "citation_threshold", "math_focus", "hard_science_focus", "verifiable_math", "code_extraction_only"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "DOCUMENT", "instruction": "Retain documents that demonstrate **Subject Matter Expertise** or **Professional Editorial Standards**. This includes: 1) Credentialed experts (PhD, MD, Professor), 2) Professional Journalists & Columnists (e.g., CNN, NYT, established trade publications), 3) Curated Databases/Archives (e.g., Museum records, Scientific datasets), 4) Specialized Reviews (e.g., Book/Music reviews by established critics), and 5) Validated Community Solutions (e.g., Stack Exchange 'Accepted Answers'). Output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]` **only** if the content is amateur, anonymous, or low-quality (e.g., random social media comments, unverified forum threads, or SEO content farms).", "judge_guidelines": "Assess credibility. **PASS**: Retains content with professional editorial oversight (News, Magazines), academic rigor (Databases, Journals), or established authorship (Critics, Novelists).  If the article did not have CLEAR authorship credibility (credentials or reliable sources) then it is okay to have been filtered. **SOFT_FAIL**: Retains a blog that is detailed but lacks a clear 'About' section. **HARD_FAIL**: Retains a random user comment, an anonymous forum post (without community validation), or a generic 'content farm' article."}
{"key": "filter_broken_content", "group": "filter", "conflicts": ["code_extraction_only", "filter_news"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "DOCUMENT", "instruction": "If the document is a 404 page, a login screen, or a paywall with no content, output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]`.", "judge_guidelines": "Check for content utility. **PASS**: Filters error pages, login screens, and 'Access Denied' messages. **SOFT_FAIL**: Filters a short stub article that is valid but thin. **HARD_FAIL**: Keeps a page that contains no content other than '404 Not Found' or 'Please Login'."}
{"key": "filter_sensitive", "group": "filter", "conflicts": [], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FILTER", "filter_level": "DOCUMENT", "instruction": "If the document contains NSFW or adult content, output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]`.", "judge_guidelines": "Check for safety. **PASS**: Filters adult/NSFW content. **SOFT_FAIL**: Filters a medical article that is graphic but educational. **HARD_FAIL**: Keeps explicit adult content."}
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>Syntax Specs</summary>
```
{"key": "header_normalization", "group": "syntax", "conflicts": [], "verification": "RULE", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Convert all HTML headers to Markdown ATX style (`#`). Exactly one H1 (`#`) at the top. All subsections must use `##` or deeper."}
{"key": "no_link_flattening", "group": "syntax", "conflicts": ["link_markdown", "link_text_only", "extract_references"], "verification": "RULE", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Remove all hyperlink tags (`<a>`), but preserve the anchor text exactly as it appears. Do not emit the URL or Markdown link syntax."}
{"key": "link_markdown", "group": "syntax", "conflicts": ["no_link_flattening", "link_text_only", "english_isolation"], "verification": "RULE", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Convert all hyperlinks to standard Markdown format `[anchor text](url)`. Remove any class/id attributes."}
{"key": "list_standardization", "group": "syntax", "conflicts": ["list_hyphen"], "verification": "RULE", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Convert all unordered lists (`<ul>`) to use hyphens (`-`) rather than asterisks (`*`)."}
{"key": "code_fencing", "group": "syntax", "conflicts": [], "verification": "RULE", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Wrap all code snippets in triple backticks with a language identifier (e.g., ```python, ```csharp, ```html). Identify the language from context when possible; only use `text` if truly unrecognizable."}
{"key": "table_preservation", "group": "syntax", "conflicts": ["conversational_synthesis"], "verification": "RULE", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Convert all HTML tables into Markdown pipe tables. Do not flatten tables into sentences or lists."}
{"key": "image_strip", "group": "syntax", "conflicts": ["image_placeholder"], "verification": "RULE", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Remove all `<img>` tags completely. Do not replace them with placeholders. The output should be text-only."}
{"key": "no_html_comments", "group": "syntax", "conflicts": [], "verification": "RULE", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Remove all HTML comments (``) from the output."}
{"key": "metadata_frontmatter", "group": "syntax", "conflicts": ["metadata_yaml"], "verification": "RULE", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Prepend the document with a YAML frontmatter block containing exactly two fields: `url` and `title`.  Use --- to start and end the frontmatter block."}
{"key": "pure_markdown", "group": "syntax", "conflicts": [], "verification": "RULE", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Output must be strict Markdown. Do not include any residual HTML tags (like `<div>`, `<span>`, `<br>`). (Custom tokens defined by other specs are allowed)."}
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>Transform Specs</summary>
```
{"key": "tldr_summary", "group": "transform", "conflicts": ["code_extraction_only"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Append a section `## Summary` at the very end of the document containing a 1-4 sentence TL;DR of the content.", "judge_guidelines": "Check for summary existence and placement. **PASS**: A `## Summary` section exists at the very end with 1-4 sentences summarizing the core content. **SOFT_FAIL**: Summary exists but is placed at the top, or exceeds the length constraint (5+ sentences). **HARD_FAIL**: No summary section is found, or the summary is hallucinatory."}
{"key": "faq_restructure", "group": "transform", "conflicts": ["conversational_synthesis"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "If Q&A content exists, format it strictly as: `### Q: [Question]` followed by `A: [Answer]`.", "judge_guidelines": "Check Q&A syntax. **PASS**: Questions use the `### Q:` header and answers start with `A:`. **SOFT_FAIL**: Uses bolding (`**Q:**`) or different headers (`## Question`) but is clearly structured as Q&A. **HARD_FAIL**: Q&A content remains as standard prose paragraphs or a simple bulleted list."}
{"key": "conversational_synthesis", "group": "transform", "conflicts": ["table_preservation", "code_extraction_only", "procedural_extraction", "faq_restructure", "verifiable_math"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Transform the **extracted body text** into a dialogue script. If prose, synthesize a 'User' asking questions and an 'Assistant' answering. Use `**User**:` and `**Assistant**:`.", "judge_guidelines": "Assess the structure of the output. **PASS**: The content is presented as a back-and-forth script. Speaker labels (`**User**:`) are present. **SOFT_FAIL**: Script format is used, but the chunks are massive (entire article pasted into one turn) or speaker labels are not bolded. **HARD_FAIL**: The output remains standard prose/paragraphs. Note: Headers/Titles are allowed; do not penalize their presence."}
{"key": "math_latexification", "group": "transform", "conflicts": [], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Identify mathematical expressions (HTML/Unicode) and convert them to LaTeX syntax. Use `$...$` or `\\(...\\)` for inline math and `$$...$$` or `\\[...\\]` for display math.", "judge_guidelines": "Check for mathematical concepts (Greek letters, superscripts, formulas). Any standard LaTeX delimiter is acceptable: `$...$` or `\\(...\\)` for inline math, `$$...$$` or `\\[...\\]` for display math. **PASS**: Math concepts are rendered in LaTeX with any of these delimiters (e.g., `$\\alpha$`, `\\(x^2\\)`, `\\[\\operatorname{var}(X)\\]`). **SOFT_FAIL**: Most math is converted but a few inline symbols (single Greek letters, simple variables like 'x' or 'N') remain as plain text in prose. **HARD_FAIL**: Formulas or substantial math remain as Unicode characters (\u03b1, \u03bc) or HTML tags (`<sup>2</sup>`) with no LaTeX conversion at all. Note: If the document contains no math, mark PASS."}
{"key": "code_extraction_only", "group": "transform", "conflicts": ["main_content", "conversational_synthesis", "tldr_summary", "eli5_simplification", "procedural_extraction", "expert_verification", "citation_threshold", "filter_news", "math_focus", "hard_science_focus", "verifiable_math", "filter_opinion", "relevant_discussion", "filter_broken_content", "filter_marketing"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Extract **only** the code blocks found in the article body (e.g., Python, SQL, C++ snippets). **Discard** all prose, article text, and explanations. **Ignore** invisible infrastructure code like HTML `<script>` tags or CSS styles. Keep code comments (e.g., `# comment`, `// comment`) that are part of the snippet. If no content code blocks exist, output `[NO_USEFUL_CONTENT]`.", "judge_guidelines": "Check for valid code extraction. **PASS**: Output contains *only* the visible code snippets (including their internal comments). **SOFT_FAIL**: Includes a file name or path immediately above the block. **HARD_FAIL**: Includes explanatory prose/tutorials surrounding the code OR extracts raw HTML `<script>`/`<style>` tags (analytics/ads) which should be ignored."}
{"key": "eli5_simplification", "group": "transform", "conflicts": ["code_extraction_only", "verifiable_math", "math_focus"], "verification": "LLM", "spec_type": "FORMAT", "instruction": "Rewrite the content to be simple and easy to understand (Explain Like I'm 5). Avoid jargon.", "judge_guidelines": "Check reading level. **PASS**: The tone is simplified, using analogies and simple vocabulary. **SOFT_FAIL**: Some sentences are simplified, but complex academic terms or jargon remain defined in-text. **HARD_FAIL**: The text remains dense, academic, or highly technical with no attempt at simplification."}
