<details>
<summary>Producing the GPT-OSS-120B Data</summary>

Now that I had my 40 specs with high accuracy at N=3 combinations (for the samples I tested, I did not test all combinations) I wanted to sample 1k spec combinations and use these for training.  I sampled the specs, but because these were all derived from the same specs I wanted to rephrase them so that the model when fine-tuned wouldn't just look for spurious textual cues to decide what the rules for rephrasing were.

Using GPT-OSS-120B I used [verbalized sampling](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01171) with this prompt:

```python
META_PROMPT_TEMPLATE = """\
Generate 5 responses to the input prompt. Each response should be approximately {target_words} words.
Return the responses in JSON format with the key: "responses"
(list of dicts). Each dictionary must include:
• text: the response string only (no explanation or extra
text).
• probability: the estimated probability from 0.0 to 1.0 of
this response given the input prompt (relative to the full
distribution).
Give ONLY the JSON object, with no explanations or extra text.

---

Rewrite the below rephraser spec in a way that maintains the meaning but is completely rephrased. Feel free to shuffle the order.  I encourage you to drop the labels that are bracketed at the start of each spec detail.  Some good rewrites will be less formal than these and others will match the tone.  Try to use diverse ways to convey the information rather than using the exact same words provided in the original spec.  Including newlines (\\n) and other formatting (enumerated lists, hyphenated bullet points, etc.) when rewriting the spec is encouraged but not required for all specs.

SPEC TO REPHRASE:
{prompt}"""
```

From the outputs I renormalized from the probabilities the llm wrote for itself and sampled from those completions to get 1 rephrase per spec.  Then I started running on the Stanford NLP cluster with the sc-loprio queue and a custom orchestrator that would deliver requests to any active nodes.  I request 2 H100s/H200s and spin up vllm on them.  Here is my current progress on generating the 200M output tokens for distillation:

```
